Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are widely used for automated annotation, yet their per-class accuracy varies widely (e.g., 12\%--98\% across 13 classroom behavior classes) and is expensive to measure: evaluating one 27B MLLM on 5{,}416 validation images takes roughly 14 hours, whereas a frozen-CLIP pass over the same images completes in about 3 minutes. No cheap signal currently ranks classes by expected MLLM annotation difficulty \emph{a priori}. Building on the AnchorProxy construct (per-class zero-shot CLIP accuracy) introduced in our companion study, this paper systematically evaluates its full-frame formulation---termed \textbf{AnchorScore} here---as an \emph{a priori} diagnostic that flags the classes MLLMs are least likely to annotate reliably.

On classroom behavior data (SCB5, 13 classes, 6 MLLMs), AnchorScore correlates with per-class MLLM accuracy (Spearman $\rho=0.769$, $p=0.002$, $n=13$). None of the alternative difficulty predictors (DINOv2, ResNet-50, SigLIP, or MLLM self-verbalized uncertainty) showed a significant class-level correlation. A cross-model consensus control suggests AnchorScore primarily captures a shared class-difficulty factor rather than a CLIP-specific signal: conditioning on the consensus of the other five MLLMs leaves a partial correlation statistically indistinguishable from zero. An independent replication on Stanford40 Actions yields a nearly identical effect ($\rho=0.817$, $p<0.001$); the association is strongest on activity-recognition data and attenuates on medical and satellite imagery.

Three practical applications follow: a deployable hybrid CLIP/MLLM routing strategy (predicted-class routing: up to $+$23~pp over CLIP-only at roughly 44\% MLLM cost savings, within a few points of the best all-MLLM pipeline), prompt disambiguation on hard classes (exploratory), and review-priority prediction for human verification. AnchorScore does not estimate exact MLLM accuracy; it provides a low-cost ranking signal that directs expensive MLLM evaluation to the classes where it is most informative.
